\chapter{Meet MuLL}
MuLL is a small, dynamically typed language for embedding in C++ and D
applications. Its core handles values, bindings, expressions, functions, and
control flow. Libraries provide the higher-level operations. The parser and
interpreter are part of \api{MuDSL.hpp} and \api{MuDSL.d}; native standard
implementations live in \api{mull/native}, and package entry points in
\api{mull/stdlib}. MuLLIER, under \api{mull/mullier}, analyzes and optimizes
each parsed unit before evaluation; Chapters 71--79 develop that pipeline and
its optional Mu3AC native backend.

\begin{mullcode}
val Printf <- G:LoadLib("printf") or G:DieWith("Import Error: $E?");
val Terminal <- G:LoadLib("terminal") or G:DieWith("Import Error: $E?");
Printf:SetMainSink(G:GetStdout());
Printf:Println("Hello, MuLL!");
\end{mullcode}

\section{Read the punctuation}
\api{val} introduces an immutable binding. The arrow \api{<-} points from
the expression to the binding receiving its value. A colon selects a member;
parentheses invoke it. Semicolons terminate declarations, assignments, return
statements, and expression statements. Braced control-flow blocks do not need
a trailing semicolon.

The built-in record \api{G} connects the language to its host. A failed
\api{LoadLib} returns \api{nil} and records an error. Because \api{or}
short-circuits, the fatal fallback runs only when the import fails. The string
marker \api{$E?} is replaced by the last import error when that string is
evaluated.

The host must register the standard native libraries before running this
example. Chapters 67 and 68 show complete embeddings. The default discovery
root is relative to the process working directory; applications can instead
pass their own absolute library roots.
